WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.437
- [Joanna] So you just traded
in your dirty old phone.

00:00:02.437 --> 00:00:04.830
(tape zipping)
(bell dinging)

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:09.830
It's now part of the $64.5
billion secondhand phone market.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.400
In 2022, 73.5 million used

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.240
and refurbished phones were
shipped in North America alone.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.420
It's an exploding business,
and that's because phone makers

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.540
and the cellular carriers
have pushed us all to.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:25.373
- Trade in.
- Trade in.

00:00:25.373 --> 00:00:26.940
- Trade in.
- Trade it for something new

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:27.930
that suits your life now.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.110
- But who's winning and
losing in this booming market?

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.130
When I asked the carriers

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.500
and Apple to show me where the phones go

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.050
and if and how they make money on them,

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:39.030
they wouldn't speak about specifics.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.250
That's why I came here to a warehouse

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.693
in the middle of New Jersey.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.550
Every week, this facility
owned by US Mobile Phones

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.980
receives tens of thousands of devices,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.740
primarily Apple from cellular carriers.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.780
- So this is three
pallets that just came in

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.480
and it should be around 3,000 iPhones.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.640
- The company then sells those phones.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.610
Some go to wholesalers in other countries,

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:03.840
others get cleaned up,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.120
put through the refurbished
process and sold

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.900
to individual buyers on sites
like Amazon or Back Market,

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.480
a popular refurbished phone marketplace.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.590
We got an iPhone 11.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:14.430
- This one's a great example

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.510
'cause it's still kind of dirty.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.840
- [Joanna] Yep, I picked out
that 128-gigabyte iPhone 11

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.580
to track the cycle and how it goes

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.620
from arriving here with a trade-in value

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:26.670
of $200 to leaving here
with a price tag of $350.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.850
To understand, we have to go
through the refurbish process.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.660
The first stop for our iPhone
11: data erasure and triage.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.940
- We take the phones, we start
connecting them over here

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.430
to these computers that are
all running special software

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:43.320
to remove any potential
customer information

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.280
from the devices.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.710
- [Joanna] After that, the
phone is put to the test.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.270
Well, many tests.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.820
You can't sell a phone that doesn't work.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.020
- [Sammy] Nice and white, no shadows.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.640
Now checking the reds.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:54.870
We're good status there, right?

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.130
So you take a screenshot,

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.810
now hit the slide switch up and down.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:58.860
Say cheese.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.540
- [Joanna] That obviously
does not look good.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:01.373
- [Sammy] That's 'cause of you.

00:02:01.373 --> 00:02:02.610
We're not gonna blame the phone for that.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.388
Say cheese.
(camera snapping)

00:02:04.388 --> 00:02:05.670
(phone beeping)
- [Joanna] Ooh.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.440
- [Sammy] So that was it checking speaker.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.450
On the front over here,
it's checking flesh.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.470
You see the light on my hands?

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:11.820
So you can say yes, it's on.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:12.653
- [Joanna] I'm really not good at this.

00:02:12.653 --> 00:02:13.890
- [Sammy] It's okay. It's day one.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.360
You're doing pretty good.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.970
The devices now do a test call.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.423
- [Joanna] Success. Our iPhone
11 passed all the tests.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.033
The next step, cleaning.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:26.880
- Toothbrush, plastic peg,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.096
microfiber cloth, foam
sponges, hand sanitizer.

00:02:30.096 --> 00:02:33.210
(tranquil music)

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:34.563
- Looks nice and clean.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.950
After that, it's onto
grading where someone looks

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.840
over the device and assigns it a grade

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.553
so they know how to price it.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.570
- [Sammy] Gonna look at the
front glass, the housing,

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.810
the back glass, if there's any scratches.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.513
- [Joanna] Success again,
our device got an A.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:55.530
And the final stop, kitting.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.320
- We're gonna take this
and put it into a kit box

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.900
so that we can get this
shipped out to our customer.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.850
- [Joanna] US Mobile
Phones has a sister company

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.290
called Back in the Box,

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:07.230
which is responsible for selling
refurbished phones directly

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:11.100
to consumers on sites like
Amazon and Back Market.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.440
Before the phone is shipped,
the company packages it

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.040
with a charging cable
and with lots of padding.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:17.880
- Drop it. Drop it.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.050
- I mean...
- You could drop it.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.377
- Our poor phone.

00:03:20.377 --> 00:03:22.136
(Sammy chuckling)

00:03:22.136 --> 00:03:23.160
(phone dropping)

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.390
Okay, so now it's ready.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.850
But how much is someone
going to pay for it?

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.740
Well, for some context,

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.320
Apple doesn't make the iPhone 11 anymore

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.950
but you can still buy a
brand new 128-gigabyte model

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:37.950
for around $500 at some retailers.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.070
Back in the Box obviously
has to undercut that.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.690
- Typically what we like to do is we like

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:45.840
to price refurbished products
depending on the grade

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.590
between 20 to 30% below its
retail price and generally,

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.630
that gives a good
discount for the consumer

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:55.410
and gives us enough margin
somewhere between 10-15%

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.930
to be able to have some money,
have some profit to then go

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.600
and buy more inventory
and support the business.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.940
- [Joanna] On its website,
Apple says the trade-in value

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:06.900
for a used, good condition
iPhone 11 is $200.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.390
Back in the Box told me
it bought the iPhone 11

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.840
from a carrier for around $250.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.300
Then after the phone was refurbished,

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.960
it listed it for $350 on Back Market,

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.810
which takes a 10% cut of that sale.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.050
Back in the Box takes home around $65,

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:26.940
but that's not all profit.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.070
- Put a ton of labor into
cleaning that device.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:32.490
There's also a charger in that
box that we have to pay for.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.760
There's also a ton of
devices that came with that,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:36.750
either had a bad battery

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.420
or at some other defect that
we have to sell at a loss.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:41.100
So we have to make enough profit

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.950
on the good ones to be
able to absorb the hit

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.570
on the bad ones.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.460
- So what did we learn here today?

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:48.990
Well, that there are more winners

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.030
than losers in this business.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:53.730
The cellular carriers win
when you trade in an old phone

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:54.870
and buy a new one.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.970
Refurbished companies
win when someone buys

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.130
a refurbished phone and consumers

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:01.320
and the environment win along the way.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.990
- The downside is maybe to the stock price

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:05.700
of the manufacturers that wanna sell

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.100
as many brand new retail products.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.990
- [Joanna] Perhaps, but even for Apple,

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:11.370
if all these phones end up

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.620
in the hands of more people, particularly

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:17.910
in developing countries, it
means growing market share

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:21.903
and more people buying into
the iPhone circle of life.

00:05:23.232 --> 00:05:27.250
(Sammy singing in foreign language)

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:28.140
(Sammy chuckling)

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:30.094
- That's all I got for you.

00:05:30.094 --> 00:05:32.427
- [Joanna] That's very good.

